.SS Data sources
.PP
\fBvmstat\fR reads from several procfs files (see \fBproc\fR(5)):
.IP \(bu 2
\fI/proc/stat\fR — CPU times, total interrupts, context switches,
fork count
.IP \(bu 2
\fI/proc/meminfo\fR — memory and swap totals
.IP \(bu 2
\fI/proc/vmstat\fR — paging and swapping rates
.IP \(bu 2
\fI/proc/diskstats\fR — per-device I/O counters (\fB\-d\fR / \fB\-D\fR)
.IP \(bu 2
\fI/proc/slabinfo\fR — slab allocator state (\fB\-m\fR; root-only,
gated by the kernel)
.SS The first line is misleading
.PP
The first line of any continuous \fBvmstat\fR run reports averages
\fIsince system boot\fR, not over the most recent interval. On a
machine that has been up for weeks, it bears no useful relation to
"what's happening right now". Use \fB\-y\fR to omit it; the
subsequent lines are the per-interval values you actually want.
.SS BI / BO units
.PP
\fIBI\fR and \fIBO\fR are computed as the per-interval rate of the
kernel's \fBpgpgin\fR / \fBpgpgout\fR counters from
\fI/proc/vmstat\fR. Those counter units are not specified in
\fBproc_vmstat\fR(5) ("Details can be found by consulting the kernel
source code"); the column is documented here as "as the kernel
reports them" rather than tied to a specific block size, and
\fB\-S\fR's unit selection does not apply.
.SS Memory and swap units
.PP
\fB\-S\fR selects the unit for memory quantities and \fBSI\fR/\fBSO\fR.
Swap counters in \fI/proc/vmstat\fR count pages; rates are multiplied
by the system page size before conversion to the selected unit.
.SS Wide mode
.PP
\fB\-w\fR expands the memory columns to twelve characters and the
process columns to four. Widths are minimums; larger values remain
visible rather than being truncated.
.SS Report fields
.PP
Numeric values in \fB\-s\fR reports are right-aligned in a minimum of
13 columns. Page-allocation counts for dma, dma32, high, movable,
normal, and pages freed remain page counts regardless of \fB\-S\fR;
a zone counter absent from the kernel is zero.
.PP
Rows recover padding from later columns after an earlier value
overflows its minimum width, without truncating any counter.
.SS Context switch rate
.PP
The first \fBcs\fR value is total context switches divided by uptime;
later values use interval deltas. The procps-ng 4.0.7 first-row
calculation using accumulated CPU ticks as divisor is not reproduced.
